   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Azerbaijan Trade Unions Confederation (AHIK) developed a policy position regarding shortcomings in the state response measures towards economic and health crises induced by the COVID-19 pandemic. The policy position was submitted to the National Council for Social Dialogue (NCSD) in August 2020. AHIK&#x2019;s active engagement with the Government and employers organizations, through available cooperation platforms, including the national Council for Social Dialogue and inter-ministerial anti-crises Council, have significantly contributed towards the advancement of decent work in the context of the COVID-19 pandemic.
 Through active engagement with the United Nations Resident Coordinator Office, AHIK provided its proposals to the Common Country Analysis (CCA) and the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF) in September-December 2020, and comments to the Government on the draft Voluntary National Review presented to the United Nations High-level Political Forum on Sustainable Development (HLPF) in March 2021. AHIK&#x2019;s active role in the UN reform and VNR processes have contributed to more effective attainment of nationally set SDGs in the areas relating to labour rights and protection of vulnerable categories of workers.   
AHIK has elaborated its policy position and submitted its proposals to the NCSD on possible extension of social protection to vulnerable categories of workers. This work has been informed by the analysis on the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on working conditions of workers in informal economy and labour platforms (February-March 2021).
AHIK submitted its proposals regarding youth employment issues to the NCSD as its contribution to the discussions on a new Decent work Country Programme (DWCP) 2021-2025 in September 2021. AHIK&#x2019;s continued interest towards facilitating decent employment conditions and work for youth has insured a strong focus of the new DWCP on emerging challenges caused by the ongoing COVID-19 pandemic. In July-August 2020, ILO provided support and policy advice to AHIK to analyse gaps in the governments&#x2019; COVID-19 crises mitigation measures. Relevant recommendations and proposals were submitted to the NCSD.

ILO delivered four regional webinars in April-July 2020 on trade union response strategies on the COVID-19 pandemic:
1. Disrupting lives, economies and societies: challenges and prospects for trade union actions
2. Stimulating economy and employment during COVID-19 pandemic
3. Supporting enterprises, jobs and incomes in times of crises
4. Relying on social dialogue for solutions in times of crises.
These webinars informed AHIK&#x2019;s policy position/proposals submitted to the NCSD and the national inter-ministerial anti-crises council.

ILO delivered a series of webinars in July and December 2020 for AHIK leaders and senior staff members on UN reform and SDGs, that supported AHIK&#x2019;s active engagement with UNRC office on CCA and UNSDCF. The same activities contributed to influence the Government&#x2019;s VNR report to UN HLPF in 2020.

Analysis of COVID-19 impact on workers in the informal economy and on digital labour platforms supported AHIK in developing its strategy and proposals in February-March 2021 on the extension of social protection to these workers. The proposals were submitted to the Government and NCSD.

ILO provided policy advice and support in July-September 2021, contributing to the development of AHIK&#x2019;s policy position on youth employment challenges in Azerbaijan. The conclusions and proposals, discussed by the NCSD in December 2021, will be reflected in the new DWCP.

A Russian translation of the ILO guide on organizing workers in the informal economy informed AHIK&#x2019;s policy decisions on extending services, protection and representation to workers in the informal economy.

